  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1028 AM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nToday and Tomorrow...\n\nWater vapor imagery and objective analysis this morning reveals a \nclosed mid- to upper-level low centered over the Mid-South, bringing \nnorthwesterly flow aloft locally. This pattern will continue to \nfilter cool air into the region, with highs peaking in the upper 30s \nand low 40s this afternoon. At the surface, a broad corridor of high \npressure is filtering into east-central Nebraska, bringing calm \nconditions and gradually clearing skies. Fog development is expected \nagain overnight. Model soundings indicate a narrow saturated \nlayer near the surface with gusty winds just above it, likely \nlimiting fog to patchy and low-lying areas. \n\nLow-level flow will transition to southerly into Friday as weak, \ntransient ridging slides across the area. This will allow high \ntemperatures to rise into the mid 40s to low 50s tomorrow. A surface \ntrough will pivot across the area Friday afternoon, bringing \nincreasing cloud cover through the day. While a few rain drops can't \nbe ruled out with this feature Friday afternoon and evening, \nmoisture will be rather limited with a dry sub-cloud layer that will \nhave to be overcome. Therefore, confidence is low that we will say \nprecipitation making it to the ground (10% chance). \n\nSaturday and Sunday...\n\nThis weekend, a shortwave trough is expected to push out of the \nFront Range and into the central Plains, bringing a cold front and \nincreasing precipitation chances with it. Brief WAA ahead of this \nsystem will push high temperatures into the mid 40s to low 50s \nSaturday before the cold front and increasing precipitation chances \nwork in Saturday afternoon and evening. Rain is expected to be the \ndominant precipitation type initially, with QPF peaking around 0.25-\n0.40\" through the period. PoPs currently peak at 75-95% Saturday \nevening, tapering off into early Sunday. \n\nCAA will filter in overnight into Sunday morning, with a few \nsnowflakes mixing in on the back side of the departing precipitation \nshield. While approximately 2/3rds of GEFS and EPS/EPS-AIFS ensemble \nmembers depict this rain/snow transition zone, model consensus \ncontinues to keep snow accumulations minimal. However, a few slick \nspots Sunday morning can't be ruled out given the this rain/snow mix \nand lingering precipitation freezing onto surfaces. On the bright \nside, temperatures are expected to gradually warm above freezing \nafter sunrise on Sunday with pockets of sunshine returning by the \nafternoon. High temperatures are expected to peak in the upper\n30s and low 40s on Sunday with brisk northwesterly winds \ngusting at 20-30 mph.\n\nMonday and Beyond...\n\nInto the work week, generally zonal flow aloft will return to the \narea, gradually increasing temperatures through the first half of \nthe work week. Highs are expected in the mid to upper 40s Monday, \nupper 40s to low 50s Tuesday, and low to mid 50s Wednesday. Long \nrange guidance depicts a weak shortwave trough pivoting across the \narea late Monday into Tuesday. Moisture will be limited with this \npassage, with precipitation unlikely.\n\nLooking ahead, a more potent shortwave trough is expected to eject \ninto the southern Plains by mid-week with additional shortwave \nenergy passing to our north. However, long range guidance continues \nto display varying solutions on the extend of moisture being pulled \ninto our region with these two features at play. PoPs currently peak \nat 15% on Thursday, we will continue to monitor trends as the system \napproaches.",
